    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "High-glycemic foods include things like white bread, sugary cereals, white rice, and most processed snacks, which are often the first to be spotted by the hungry, hungry, hungry goblins in the pantry. Low-glycemic options are usually whole grains like quinoa, oats, brown rice, and legumes, which are the sturdy, slow-moving tanks of the food world.\n\nFor your client briefing, you can suggest swapping a slice of white toast for a slice of whole-grain toast, or replacing a bowl of sugary oatmeal with steel-cut oats topped with berries. These swaps help the client's body avoid the frantic energy spikes that come from high-glycemic choices.\n\nYou might also mention that fruits like bananas and grapes are higher on the glycemic scale, while berries and apples are lower, making them better choices for a stable afternoon snack. By focusing on these specific swaps, the client can easily plan meals that keep their blood sugar steady.\n\nIt's also helpful to remind them that cooking methods matter; for instance, boiling potatoes makes them higher glycemic than roasting them, so suggesting different preparation styles can be a game-changer. Ultimately, the goal is to help the client",
